    "q-81787": {
      "dom_id": "step-q81787",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Let $A$  be the set of all multiples of $3$ greater than $0$ and let $B$  be the set of all multiples of $6$ greater than $0.$ What is $A-B?$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiples of $3$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA=\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB=\\{6,12,18,24,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $A-B$ is the *difference* of $A$ and $B,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $A$ but *not* in $B.$\n\nThe elements of $A$ that are also in $B$ are $6,12,18,24,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $6$). So, we must remove these elements from $A.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-B & =\\{3,6,9,12,15,18,21,24,…\\}-\\{6,12,18,24,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{3,9,15,21,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
