    "q-105229": {
      "dom_id": "step-q105229",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Let $A$  be the set of all multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ and let $B$  be the set of all multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0.$  What is $B∖A?$",
      "worked_solution": "The multiples of $4$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nA=\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLikewise, the multiples of $6$ that are greater than $0$ are\n\n$$\nB=\\{6,12,18,24,30,36,…\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, $B∖A$ is the *difference* of $B$ and $A,$ which means that we want to find the elements that are in $B$ but *not* in $A.$\n\nThe elements of $B$ that are also in $A$ are $12,24,36,$ and so on (i.e. multiples of $12$). So, we must remove these elements from $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B∖A & =B-A \\\\  & =\\{6,12,18,24,30,36,…\\}-\\{4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,36,…\\} \\\\  & =\\{6,18,30,42,54,…\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
